[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a moment, then walk me through how you'd approach this.

[candidate · 0:05] Let me take a second to structure this.

So revenue is up 15% but margin collapsed from 24% to 6% — that's a huge drop, so this is almost certainly a cost-side story rather than a demand story. I'd break profit into revenue and costs. On revenue I'd want to know whether the 15% growth came from new stores, more transactions per store, or higher prices. On costs I'd split into COGS — coffee beans, milk, food, cups — and operating costs like labor, rent, utilities, and corporate overhead.

One thing I'd want to dig into early on the COGS side is waste and spoilage. Specialty coffee chains carry a lot of perishables — milk, pastries, prepared food — and if they've expanded the food menu or opened stores that aren't hitting volume, spoilage can quietly eat margin without showing up in unit prices.

Do we have a cost breakdown as a percent of revenue for both years?
